&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;DateRange&lt;/code&gt; type comes from the package's public calendar types. The shared &lt;code&gt;isValidIsoDate&lt;/code&gt; helper narrows values to plain calendar dates and rejects timestamps and impossible dates such as February 29 in a non-leap year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After validation, comparing normalized date strings is sufficient for this dataset. Each string has the same year-month-day format, so lexical order is chronological order. That is why &lt;code&gt;filterOrders&lt;/code&gt; can compare &lt;code&gt;bookedOn&lt;/code&gt; directly with &lt;code&gt;from&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;to&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Revo UI owns the date mechanics: &lt;code&gt;addDays&lt;/code&gt; uses UTC arithmetic, &lt;code&gt;startOfMonth&lt;/code&gt; resolves the first day of the month, and &lt;code&gt;diffInCalendarDays&lt;/code&gt; returns the signed day difference. The example adds one because its reporting policy includes both endpoints. &lt;code&gt;formatCalendarDateDisplay&lt;/code&gt; formats a civil date without shifting it to another day in the browser's timezone; the &lt;code&gt;formatDate&lt;/code&gt; wrapper chooses the report locale. For example, &lt;code&gt;formatDate('2026-11-16', 'fr-FR')&lt;/code&gt; produces &lt;code&gt;16 nov. 2026&lt;/code&gt;. The 31-day limit, permitted reporting bounds, and invoice filtering remain application rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This approach does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; define a timestamp-reporting policy. If your rows contain instants such as &lt;code&gt;2026-11-16T23:30:00Z&lt;/code&gt;, calculate business-timezone boundaries on the server. A half-open interval—start inclusive, next-day start exclusive—is usually easier to express than “23:59:59.999.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fixture stores money in cents, which keeps the additions in this small example integral. Financial systems may need a decimal money type and explicit currency rules beyond this tutorial.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep draft and applied dates separate
&lt;/h2&gt;

