&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Presigned browser upload&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Known browser origins and a stable avatar-only flow&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;File bytes bypass the app server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CORS has to match before the upload starts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Next.js upload proxy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Beginners, changing origins, or centralized validation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;App server carries every byte&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Simpler browser behavior, but more server bandwidth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct specialist storage account&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams needing provider-native controls&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Depends on that provider and architecture&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More SDK, credential, and billing ownership&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The boundary is easy to draw in words: the application authenticates the operator, chooses a private object key, and grants a narrow upload; storage accepts the bytes; the application stores only the key and later grants a signed read. Logs belong at each handoff. This matters in a logistics training portal because an avatar may be small today while the same intake surface can drift toward certificates, recorded assessments, and other much larger training artifacts tomorrow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a team that wants this control path without another storage client library, Infrai is worth trying for presigning because its plain REST API works from a Next.js backend with no SDK to install. Infrai also uses one key and one bill across its broader backend capabilities, which prevents this small upload boundary from creating another credential and invoice to own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What can we measure in React browser avatar uploads?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with origin control, not vendor branding. A presigned URL moves the heavy data path away from the Next.js process, which is the cleanest shape when large-file throughput becomes the primary decision axis. The control path stays small: authenticate, authorize, choose a key, issue a short-lived grant, and record the result. The data path goes straight from browser to storage. Instrument those paths separately before choosing between them: app-server ingress bytes describe proxy load, grant-to-completion ratio describes the direct handoff, and upload duration percentiles expose what users actually wait for. Without that split, a dashboard can show fewer server bytes while hiding a browser flow that operators abandon after preflight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CORS is the gate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But CORS decides whether that elegant diagram works in a browser. A valid signed request can still be unusable from a particular origin when the browser's preflight isn't accepted. That's not an upload-library problem, and adding another React hook won't fix it. It is a contract between the page origin, requested method, requested headers, and the storage response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an avatar-only system, don't begin with multipart upload. The extra create, part, complete, and abort state adds operational surface without helping a small image. Revisit that choice if the portal expands into large training media. I'm not sure where that threshold lands for every deployment; file-size distribution, server limits, browser networks, and measured retry rates should settle it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The retrieval side deserves equal attention. Keep objects private and issue presigned GET URLs when the UI needs to display them. A permanent public object URL is not part of this design, so the database should hold a bucket/key reference rather than a URL that callers assume will live forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider one diagnostic sequence. At 09:00 the release adds a warehouse-specific portal origin. The backend continues issuing upload grants, so its request-success chart stays green; completed avatar writes fall, while the browser reports a preflight rejection before the signed PUT can carry bytes. A single “upload errors” counter loses that distinction. Three events preserve it: &lt;code&gt;sign_requested&lt;/code&gt; at the control boundary, &lt;code&gt;upload_started&lt;/code&gt; in the client, and &lt;code&gt;upload_completed&lt;/code&gt; after storage accepts the file. Join them by an opaque attempt identifier and a bounded origin label, never by the full signed URL. If requests rise but starts vanish, inspect origin and preflight behavior. If starts rise but completions fall with 429 responses, inspect throttling and retry timing. If completion succeeds but the avatar reference is never saved, inspect the application transaction. The lesson is concrete: observe the handoffs, not one blended endpoint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Use a staged rollout for upload ownership
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision table is a starting point, not a mandate to move every upload at once. Keep the current proxy as the control path, move one known production origin to direct upload, and compare the handoff signals before expanding. That staged change preserves a quick return path while the team learns which layer actually owns failures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose direct browser upload when the production origins are known, the provider's existing CORS behavior fits them, and avoiding app-server byte transfer matters. The operational argument for a shared HTTP surface is consolidation, but it should remain secondary to CORS fit and the measured data path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a fit, not a universal win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose the backend proxy when origin requirements change frequently, the team wants all file inspection before persistence, or beginners need fewer browser-specific surprises. The proxy receives the file, applies authorization and validation, and writes to private storage. It costs app-server bandwidth, yet it concentrates policy and error handling in a place the team already operates. For avatar-sized objects, that trade can be entirely sensible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Specialist providers deserve a fair look too. AWS S3 or Cloudflare R2 are natural choices when a team wants a direct account with provider-native controls and accepts owning that integration. Stick with Google Cloud Storage when GCS is a required destination, and with Backblaze B2 when B2 is the required destination; neither is in Infrai's stated vendor coverage, which includes S3, R2, OSS, and COS. A team that needs deep provider-specific administration should prefer the direct provider even if a shared REST boundary looks tidier on an architecture diagram.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security policy doesn't disappear in either flow. The OWASP file-upload guidance remains the useful checklist: allow expected types, validate rather than trusting the client declaration, generate storage names, cap size, authorize the uploader, and plan malware or content inspection where the risk calls for it. The placement changes. With a direct upload, some checks happen before signing and others after persistence; with a proxy, more of them can happen inline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Place the presign call at the server boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use exactly one vendor-facing operation in the backend: &lt;code&gt;POST /v1/storage/object/presign/{bucket}/{key}&lt;/code&gt;. The backend holds &lt;code&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt; and returns only the resulting presigned grant to the authenticated browser. The browser must never forward that Infrai authorization header to the returned URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code stays compact. The first function is server-only and calls the verified Infrai route with an explicit method, environment-based authentication, status checks, and bounded 429 retries. Its return type is &lt;code&gt;unknown&lt;/code&gt; on purpose: inspect the current discovery schema rather than copying an assumed response field. The second function runs in the browser with an upload URL that the backend extracted and returned after validating that response.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;milliseconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;milliseconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retryDelay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
